From c3474e624dbfae7261413c6f1c3c3e16d6824634 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Flores Digest Automation <frank@floresinsuranceagency.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 03:08:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/11] Dedupe the stylesheet: 14 rules were pasted twice

Tonight's send was REFUSED by the prune_css guard at 16,391 bytes, 7 over
Gmail's ~16,384 single-block cap. The guard did its job -- nothing went out
unstyled -- but the sheet had to shrink before the digest could be sent.

The cause is not growth. report_template.html carries fourteen rules twice,
byte-identical, at unit indices 1-13 and 20-22 against 217-237: *, body,
.header, h1, .subtitle, h2, .panel, table, th, .num, .trg, .trw, .trc and
.s2d-card .rep .dot. That is a block of base rules pasted a second time, and
it is 810 bytes -- which is most of the 1,987-byte jump from 15,864 to 17,851
that HANDOFF 11 s4 attributes to "one merge".

Removing the EARLIER copy of each pair cannot change a computed value: same
selector, same specificity, byte-identical declarations, so the later copy
already won every one of them. Keeping the last is the safe direction --
anything declared between the two copies still gets overridden as before.

Verified against tonight's rendered ops document: the pruned sheet goes
16,391 -> 15,581 with 803 bytes of headroom, and every class that had a rule
before still has one after. The staff document prunes to 15,226. For scale,
the last known-good sheet (delivered 2026-08-26) was 15,864.

prune_css also now drops byte-identical duplicate rules at send time, keeping
the last. That is a guard, not the fix -- the template is deduped above -- so
that the next stray paste costs a log line instead of a refused send.

Not touched: GMAIL_CLIP_BYTES, the overflow shedding path, and anything on the
contact-rate side. This is a rendering change only.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y1XuCEz7biFWMeGktdzPGV
---
 render_report.py              | 23 +++++++++++++++++++++--
 template/report_template.html |  2 +-
 2 files changed, 22 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/render_report.py b/render_report.py
index 15064f6..37368e8 100644
--- a/render_report.py
+++ b/render_report.py
@@ -71,10 +71,29 @@ def prune_css(html, log=None):
         classes = re.findall(r"\.([A-Za-z0-9_-]+)", sel)
         if sel.strip().startswith("@") or not classes or any(c in used for c in classes):
             keep.append(unit)
-    out = "".join(keep)
+    # Drop byte-identical duplicate rules, keeping the LAST occurrence.
+    # A rule repeated verbatim is dead weight: same selector, same specificity,
+    # same declarations, so the later copy already wins and deleting the earlier
+    # one cannot change a single computed value. Keeping the last (not the
+    # first) is the safe direction -- anything declared between the two copies
+    # still gets overridden exactly as it did before.
+    #
+    # This is a guard, not the fix. The 810 bytes it found on 2026-08-31 were a
+    # block of base rules pasted twice into report_template.html, which is what
+    # took the sheet from 15,864 to 17,851 "in one merge" (HANDOFF 11 s4). The
+    # template has been deduped; this keeps the next stray paste from silently
+    # eating the headroom again.
+    last = {}
+    for i, unit in enumerate(keep):
+        last[unit] = i
+    deduped = [u for i, u in enumerate(keep) if last[u] == i]
+    dups = len(keep) - len(deduped)
+
+    out = "".join(deduped)
     if log:
         log(f"  css pruned {len(css):,} -> {len(out):,} bytes "
-            f"({len(units) - len(keep)} rules unused in this document)")
+            f"({len(units) - len(keep)} rules unused in this document"
+            + (f", {dups} duplicate rules dropped" if dups else "") + ")")
     if len(out) > STYLE_BLOCK_LIMIT:
         raise SystemExit(
             f"REFUSING TO SEND: stylesheet is {len(out):,} bytes, over Gmail's "
