Frank, 2026-09-01: "theres 2 outbounds to 2 numbers. he got a hold on the second
one, it 2 dials, one unique number/contact."

The previous commit dropped the duplicate outright and took its attempt with it.
Mike's total_dials fell 57 -> 56, erasing a call he really made. Wrong: he
dialled twice, he reached one person.

The model already had the right shape and I did not follow it. One number
dialled twice is ONE call_volume entry carrying attempts=2. A second number for
the same person is the same thing, so its attempts now move onto the surviving
row before the duplicate is dropped:

    +1 917-804-1091   252s   live, attempts 2   <- survivor, carries both dials
    +1 631-276-3801    67s   dropped as duplicate

Mike now reads 53 unique / 57 dials / 9 live / 17.0%. Before this fix he read 53
/ 56 / 9, and as sent on Sunday 54 / 57 / 12 / 22.2%.

verify_finalize.py needed the same distinction and caught the error itself --
it flagged total_dials 57 against a recomputed 56 the moment the attempt went
missing. A dial dropped as a DUPLICATE LEAD keeps its attempt in the
recomputation; every other drop reason does not, because a service/renewal call
is not new-business activity at all. That asymmetry is now explicit in both
places rather than implied.

Also takes the day as an argument, defaulting to today in Arizona. It was
hardcoded to 2026-08-25 (HANDOFF_11 s7), so anyone running it against a newer
build silently reconciled a stale file -- or, in a fresh container, died on a
missing metrics file. `python3 verify_finalize.py 2026-08-31` now prints the day
it is checking.

